WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:04.839
Bad acting, worse writing, and directors put
on blast.

00:00:04.839 --> 00:00:08.230
Some movies are so bad, even their stars hate
being in them.

00:00:08.865 --> 00:00:12.490
Though it suffered from bad reviews upon its
initial release, "The Sound of Music" has

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:15.868
gone on to become one of the most beloved
movie musicals of all time.

00:00:15.868 --> 00:00:20.850
Families regularly gather 'round their televisions
to watch it, the songs remain catchy, and

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.920
Julie Andrews' performance as Maria is an
iconic piece of work, matched only by her

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:26.630
role as Mary Poppins.

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:30.449
That said, one of the film's other stars has
never been a fan.

00:00:30.449 --> 00:00:34.210
Christopher Plummer has spent decades reminding
viewers that he never liked "The Sound of

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:39.770
Music," referring to the film as "The Sound
of Mucus," "S&amp;M" or simply "that movie" at

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:41.430
various points over the years.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.320
In 2010, he told The Boston Globe:

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.550
"I was a bit bored with the character.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:49.250
Although we worked hard enough to make him
interesting, it was a bit like flogging a

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:50.250
dead horse.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.060
And the subject matter is not mine.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.969
I mean, it can't appeal to every person in
the world.

00:00:54.969 --> 00:00:56.039
It's not my cup of tea.”

00:00:56.039 --> 00:01:01.180
Before his death in 2021, Plummer always made
his thoughts on the film clear — albeit,

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.010
while also acknowledging that people everywhere
love it.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:06.580
In a 2015 interview with Vanity Fair, he said:

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:10.790
"As cynical as I always was about 'The Sound
of Music,' I do respect that it is a bit of

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:14.610
relief from all the gunfire and car chases
you see these days.

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:17.320
It's sort of wonderfully, old-fashionedly
universal."

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.060
By the time the film that would be released
as "Star Wars" came into his life, Sir Alec

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:26.229
Guinness was already a legend — and that's
why director George Lucas wanted him.

00:01:26.229 --> 00:01:29.979
The young filmmaker hoped the name and reputation
of the star would bolster his film, which

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:34.680
largely starred unknowns, and offered Sir
Alec a chunk of the film's earnings in exchange.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.500
Guinness, thinking it was just another payday,
took the job.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.930
Then "Star Wars" became "A New Hope" and launched
the most powerful franchise in the galaxy;

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:46.960
a lot of people involved in the production
were happy about it, but Guinness was not.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.710
Yes, "Star Wars" made him a lot of money,
but he famously couldn't stand the film itself.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:56.299
He refused to open any mail related to the
film, and even asked George Lucas to limit

00:01:56.299 --> 00:02:00.299
future appearances of the Obi-Wan Kenobi character
because he hated the dialogue.

00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:03.200
"You don't need to see his identification."

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.000
"We don't need to see his identification."

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.220
"These aren't the droids you're looking for."

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:12.420
Most famously, Guinness once told a young
fan, who claimed to have seen "Star Wars"

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:16.990
many times, that he'd only sign an autograph
for him if he promised to never watch the

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:17.990
movie again.

00:02:18.261 --> 00:02:22.892
Just about any list of the greatest sci-fi
films ever made has to include "Blade Runner."

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:28.200
Ridley Scott's 1982 classic about a cop tracking
rogue synthetic humans across a futuristic

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:33.360
Los Angeles is still beloved today for its
visuals, its performances, and the many mysteries

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.060
buried within its story structure.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:40.260
"Blade Runner" is also remembered for overcoming
so-so reviews, extensive financial turmoil,

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.030
and development hurdles.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:45.910
It's a movie that persevered, against all
odds, to become a cult classic.

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:48.960
"Quite an experience to live in fear, isn't
it?

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:54.030
That's what it is to be a slave."

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:58.670
While frequent re-edits, re-releases, sequels
and spinoffs have kept new audiences finding

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:03.200
the film and falling in love, star Harrison
Ford apparently isn't one of them.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.950
In a 1999 interview with SFGATE, Ford said
that no matter which cut of "Blade Runner"

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:10.090
he watches, he responds the same way.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:12.499
"I didn't like the movie one way or the other.

00:03:12.499 --> 00:03:15.730
[...] I played a detective who did not have
any detecting to do.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:19.160
In terms of how I related to the material,
I found it very difficult.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:22.200
There was stuff that was going on that was
really nuts."

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:27.780
Despite these struggles, Ford eventually did
return to the role of Deckard for "Blade Runner 2049."

00:03:28.134 --> 00:03:32.610
Sometimes actors dislike movies they made
because they didn't like the script, or they

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:36.840
just did it for the money, or they were overwhelmed
by the fame thrust upon them.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:41.141
Then there's Crispin Glover, who has his own
very specific reasons for disliking "Back

00:03:41.141 --> 00:03:42.141
to the Future."

00:03:42.141 --> 00:03:44.080
"What are you doing, George?"

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.230
"I'm writing this down, this is good stuff."

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:47.230
"Yeah, okay."

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:48.950
In a 2012 interview with The A.V.

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:53.579
Club looking back on his career, Glover went
into a lot of detail about why he didn't appear

00:03:53.579 --> 00:03:56.700
in either of the film's sequels as George
McFly.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.890
It's complicated, and it actually helped set
legal precedent.

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:03.720
But according to Glover, a lot of it started
with his own dislike of the film's ending,

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:08.340
when Marty McFly goes back to 1985 and finds
that his parents are happy, and his father

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:10.970
is a successful and wealthy sci-fi author.

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:13.910
For Glover, that kind of ending just sends
the wrong message.

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:14.910
He said:

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:18.380
"I think there would be a better message if,
instead of the son character pumping his fist

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:22.570
in the air or whatever, [...] if instead the
reward was that the mother and father characters

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:23.590
are in love with each other.

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:25.630
And that there’s the potential that money
comes in.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:30.240
I think [equating their new riches with moral
success] is a bad message."

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:35.270
For Glover, the riches as reward ending of
the film only further served corporate interests.

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:38.134
Director Robert Zemeckis, however, did not
seem to agree.

00:04:38.134 --> 00:04:42.411
Kate Winslet was already an accomplished young
actor by the time "Titanic" came around, but

00:04:42.411 --> 00:04:46.470
James Cameron's film about the sinking of
the legendary ocean liner made her into an

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:47.990
international superstar.

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:52.400
Though she's since gone on to many other roles,
and even won Oscars for her non-"Titanic"

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.780
work, Winslet will forever be known in the
eyes of many as the lovestruck Rose, immortalized

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:00.449
in memes and on basic cable for years to come.

00:05:00.449 --> 00:05:05.139
That said, when the time came for a "Titanic"
3D re-release, Winslet admitted that she actually

00:05:05.139 --> 00:05:07.560
doesn't like watching the film very much.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:09.320
It's not Cameron's filmmaking, though.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:10.740
It's her own performance.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:11.950
She told The Telegraph:

00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:14.300
"Every single scene, I'm like 'Really, really?

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.120
You did it like that?'

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:17.680
Oh my God.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:20.010
[...] Even my American accent, I can't listen
to it.

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:21.010
It's awful.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:24.979
[...] I have a hard time watching any of my
performances, but watching 'Titanic' I was

00:05:24.979 --> 00:05:27.060
just like, 'Oh God, I want to do that again.'"

00:05:27.481 --> 00:05:31.051
"I will never let go, Jack.

00:05:31.584 --> 00:05:33.963
I'll never let go."

00:05:33.974 --> 00:05:37.810
"Love Actually" is one of those films that
did well when initially released, but has

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:40.940
grown its audience by leaps and bounds in
the years since.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:44.850
The romantic comedy anthology about various
Londoners and their relationships in the weeks

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:49.289
leading up to Christmas has become a holiday
staple for many, continuing to draw new fans

00:05:49.289 --> 00:05:52.420
through annual theatrical showings and Christmas
movie marathons.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:56.720
That doesn't mean everyone who worked on the
film has to like it, however.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:00.759
While he acknowledges the film's impact, and
even agreed to return for the Red Nose Day

00:06:00.759 --> 00:06:05.560
reunion special in 2017, Andrew Lincoln has
admitted in interviews that he was never too

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:10.000
comfortable with his own place in the film
as Mark, the lovesick man who professes undying

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.100
affection to his best friend's wife.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:13.380
He told Entertainment Weekly:

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:17.920
"In one of the most romantic movies of all
time, I got to play the only guy who doesn't

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:18.920
get the girl.

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:22.980
The story is set up like a prism looking at
all the different qualities of love.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:24.380
Mine was unrequited.

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:26.736
So I got to be this weird stalker guy."

00:06:26.901 --> 00:06:31.750
In 2007, Katherine Heigl was an up-and-coming
actor still working on making the big leap

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:34.160
from TV stardom to movie fame.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:38.039
She got a major break in the form of Judd
Apatow's "Knocked Up," a comedy about the

00:06:38.039 --> 00:06:39.860
fallout from a one-night-stand pregnancy.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:45.000
"Knocked Up" was a major success both critically
and commercially, helped make Seth Rogen into

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:49.860
a comedic star, and further cemented Apatow
as one of the genre's guiding lights.

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:54.868
For Heigl, though she enjoyed making the film,
it was also an unpleasant story about an uptight woman.

00:06:54.868 --> 00:06:55.868
"Do not make fun of me.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:07:00.280
Okay, I am hormonal, I am terrified, and I
am falling apart.

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:02.610
So stop treating everything like it's a big
joke."

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:04.390
She told Vanity Fair in 2016:

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:06.180
"It was a little sexist.

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:10.910
It paints the women as shrews, as humorless
and uptight, and it paints the men as lovable,

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:12.740
goofy, fun-loving guys.

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:16.629
It exaggerated the characters, and I had a
hard time with it, on some days.

00:07:16.629 --> 00:07:19.890
[...] Ninety-eight percent of the time it
was an amazing experience, but it was hard

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:21.726
for me to love the movie."

00:07:21.726 --> 00:07:26.090
Robert Pattinson was one of several young
actors who had overwhelming fame thrust upon

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:28.800
them, seemingly overnight, with the original
"Twilight" film.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.890
"The Batman" brought him back to franchise
work, but Pattinson has proven himself to

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:34.990
be predominantly arthouse-minded.

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:38.280
He's not just a pretty face — he's a capable
actor.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:42.830
Based on Stephenie Meyer's massively popular
novels of the same name, the five "Twilight"

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:45.030
films drew a rabid fanbase to Pattinson.

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:49.590
And while he's well aware of what the movies
did for him in terms of status, he was never

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:51.460
actually a fan of the movies themselves.

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:56.370
In 2011, he admitted to Vanity Fair that it
was "weird" to be "kind of representing something

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:57.800
you don't particularly like."

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:01.169
"You better hold on tight, spider monkey."

00:08:01.169 --> 00:08:05.009
Asked in a Moviefone interview if he'd go
see a "Twilight" film if he wasn't in them,

00:08:05.009 --> 00:08:06.009
he responded:

00:08:06.009 --> 00:08:07.819
"[I] would just mindlessly hate it."

00:08:07.819 --> 00:08:11.090
In the years since, however, he seems to have
warmed to the films.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:13.159
He told USA TODAY in 2019:

00:08:13.159 --> 00:08:17.199
"Now the intensity has died down and it's
just very warm memories."

00:08:17.199 --> 00:08:21.349
Daniel Radcliffe's life changed forever when
he was cast as the title character in the

00:08:21.349 --> 00:08:23.690
"Harry Potter" series of films, based on J.K.

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:26.560
Rowling's mega-bestseller series of books.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:30.599
Radcliffe played Potter in eight different
films across an entire decade of his life,

00:08:30.599 --> 00:08:33.029
quite literally growing up on the big screen.

00:08:33.029 --> 00:08:36.810
The actor has fond memories of his time as
the boy wizard, and always seems happy to

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:39.310
share memories of making the "Potter" films.

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:42.890
But there is little doubt, however, which
film is his least favorite to watch.

00:08:42.890 --> 00:08:45.200
In 2014, he told the Daily Mail:

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:49.070
"It's hard to watch a film like 'Harry Potter
And The Half-Blood Prince,' because I'm just

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:50.380
not very good in it.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:51.380
I hate it.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:55.540
My acting is very one-note and I can see I
got complacent and what I was trying to do

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:56.940
just didn't come across."

00:08:57.183 --> 00:09:01.959
"The Help" is considered by some to be among
the best period dramas of the 2010s, and it

00:09:01.959 --> 00:09:03.400
earned widespread acclaim.

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:07.430
Focusing on the interpersonal relationships
between Black women working for white people

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:11.720
in Jackson, Mississippi, in 1963 — at the
height of the Civil Rights Movement — it

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:15.300
starred Viola Davis in an Academy Award-nominated
lead role.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:19.750
Today, it may be remembered as one of her
most celebrated roles, but nevertheless she's

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:23.519
unhappy with the film and says she was reluctant
to take the part.

00:09:23.519 --> 00:09:25.570
She told The New York Times in 2018:

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:29.589
"I just felt that at the end of the day that
it wasn't the voices of the maids that were

00:09:29.589 --> 00:09:30.589
heard.

00:09:30.589 --> 00:09:33.529
[...] I know that if you do a movie where
the whole premise is, I want to know what

00:09:33.529 --> 00:09:38.280
it feels like to work for white people and
to bring up children in 1963, I want to hear

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:39.850
how you really feel about it.

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:42.020
I never heard that in the course of the movie."

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:46.250
"Once I told the truth about that, I felt
free."

00:09:48.089 --> 00:09:52.339
It's likely few people would know who Megan
Fox was had she not starred in Michael Bay's

00:09:52.339 --> 00:09:54.370
"Transformers" in 2007.

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:58.608
The film introduced the Transformers to a
new generation, and it introduced Fox to the world.

00:09:58.608 --> 00:10:00.688
"Why doesn't my little bunny just hop in the
backseat?"

00:10:01.334 --> 00:10:06.034
"Oh god, I can't even tell you how much I'm
not your little bunny."

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:10.360
In the film, Fox plays the love interest of
Shia LaBeouf, who gets caught up in the Earthbound

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:14.120
adventures of Optimus Prime and crew when
they show up on their doorstep.

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:17.980
Since "Transformers" and its sequel brought
Fox a great deal of fame, you'd think it would

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:21.920
be at the top of her list of favorite projects;
that is not the case.

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:25.730
Fox was let go from the production of the
third "Transformers" movie, primarily due

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:27.620
to a falling out with Bay.

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:31.680
She told Wonderland in 2009 that Bay was "a
nightmare to work with," saying:

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:33.120
"He's like Napoleon.

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:36.160
[...] He wants to be like Hitler on his sets,
and he is.

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:38.600
So he's a nightmare to work for."

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:42.550
Following something of a public feud, Fox
and Bay reconciled, even working together

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:45.810
in the rebooted "Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles"
films.

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:49.970
Looking back on "Transformers" and its sequel,
Fox has said she hates her acting.

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:52.060
She told Entertainment Weekly in 2009:

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:55.750
"People are well aware that this is not a
movie about acting.

00:10:55.750 --> 00:11:00.180
Once you realize that, it becomes almost fun
because you can be in the moment and go, 'All

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:02.060
right, I know that when he calls 'Action!'

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:05.600
I'm either going to be running or screaming,
or both.'"

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:10.370
Ryan Reynolds has starred in several high-profile
superhero movies, but he only truly regrets

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:12.360
one: "Green Lantern."

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:16.490
Intended to launch a franchise of DC Comics
films, Reynolds was at the center of it all,

00:11:16.490 --> 00:11:20.029
and anticipation was high while the film was
in production.

00:11:20.029 --> 00:11:24.420
Cast as Hal Jordan, the cocky test pilot who
stumbles upon an alien that bestows upon him

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:29.580
a Green Lantern power ring, the film had all
the right names and story beats of the comics.

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:33.779
But all the anticipation landed with a thud,
collapsing in a brightly-lit CGI mess that

00:11:33.779 --> 00:11:38.770
didn't even include a real suit; the movie
did so poorly at the box-office that it upended

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:40.350
DC's plans at the time.

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:43.950
In the years since, Reynolds has not been
shy at joining in on the joke.

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:47.839
The actor has mocked his performance and various
aspects of the film on multiple occasions.

00:11:47.839 --> 00:11:51.570
"And please don't make the super suit green.

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:53.180
Or animated."

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:57.630
Reynolds doubled down in "Deadpool 2" by going
back in time and shooting himself in the head

00:11:57.630 --> 00:12:00.459
just as he's about to accept the role of Hal
Jordan.

00:12:00.459 --> 00:12:04.790
He's gone on talk shows, appeared in commercials,
and tweeted endlessly about the full scope

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:06.950
of "Green Lantern's" awfulness.

00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:09.040
Reynolds told Entertainment Weekly in 2016:

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:14.300
"It also fell victim to the process in Hollywood,
which is like poster first, release date second,

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:15.300
script last."

00:12:15.668 --> 00:12:20.690
"Grease" was a massive hit that turbo-charged
the careers of Olivia Newton-John, John Travolta,

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:22.110
and others.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:26.120
The film's sequel was an often forgettable
pile of celluloid — except for the fact

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:28.920
that it gave the world one Michelle Pfeiffer.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:33.300
Marking her largest role to date at the time,
"Grease 2" revisited Rydell High School some

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:37.570
two years after the previous film, this time
depicting British student Michael and the

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:41.850
object of his affections, Stephanie, the leader
of a girl's gang that only allows its members

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:44.010
to date greasers... for reasons.

00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:48.910
"Grease 2" gave Pfeiffer a chance to show
her stuff, singing and dancing and taking

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:51.390
a big leap towards becoming a household name.

00:12:51.390 --> 00:12:56.140
But it is also regarded by some to be among
the worst musicals of all time.

00:12:56.140 --> 00:12:58.339
She told Hollywood.com in 2014:

00:12:58.339 --> 00:13:02.560
"I hated that film with a vengeance and could
not believe how bad it is.

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:05.470
At the time, I was young and didn't know better."

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:09.400
Pfeiffer also didn't think she'd get the part,
calling her casting a "total fluke."

00:13:09.816 --> 00:13:14.260
"High School Musical" was a massive success,
and it launched the career of multiple stars,

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:15.699
including Zac Efron.

00:13:15.699 --> 00:13:20.330
Not only was the film a hit, but it also spawned
two sequels, and Efron starred in both of

00:13:20.330 --> 00:13:22.920
them, playing Troy Bolton to widespread popularity.

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:27.531
Efron was a teenage heartthrob who could sing,
dance, and look good doing it.

00:13:27.531 --> 00:13:28.363
"Whoa.

00:13:28.363 --> 00:13:29.433
Timeout on that one."

00:13:29.629 --> 00:13:34.050
After he put those films in his rearview mirror,
Efron got to work playing more diverse roles.

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:39.060
While "Musical" made him a star, the way Efron
sees it, his most awkward stage in life was

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:41.880
also immortalized for generations of teens.

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:45.990
He discussed how the role made him feel in
a 2021 interview with Men's Journal, and said:

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:50.790
"I step back and look at myself, and I still
want to kick that guy's ass sometimes.

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:54.180
[...] He's done some kind of cool things with
some cool people, [...] but I mean, he's still

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:56.290
just that f---ing kid from ['High School Musical']."

00:13:56.290 --> 00:14:00.990
By this point, Efron has done a pretty good
job leaving Troy Bolton in his past, and he's

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:06.009
also become a documentarian; he hosts a travel
show called "Down to Earth with Zac Efron,"

00:14:06.009 --> 00:14:09.572
which sees him traveling the globe in search
of healthy lifestyles.

00:14:09.572 --> 00:14:14.850
Bob Hoskins was an exceptional actor, lending
power and presence to numerous iconic roles.

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:17.730
But there's one iconic role that didn't work
out so well.

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:22.430
In fact, it's sometimes cited among the worst
movies ever made; it was also the first video

00:14:22.430 --> 00:14:24.810
game adaptation ever put to film.

00:14:24.810 --> 00:14:29.240
Long before Chris Pratt ever said "It's-a-me,"
Hoskins took on the role of "Mario Mario"

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:31.589
in "Super Mario Bros." in 1993.

00:14:31.589 --> 00:14:35.110
His first and last name being the same in
the film was the first of many mistakes made

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:36.530
in its production.

00:14:36.530 --> 00:14:40.471
With the possible exception of the costumes
for Mario and Luigi, little about the movie

00:14:40.471 --> 00:14:42.430
relates to the source material.

00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:43.261
"Aliens?

00:14:43.261 --> 00:14:45.072
We gotta deal with aliens too?"

00:14:45.072 --> 00:14:47.060
"Luigi, we're the aliens."

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:48.110
"We are?

00:14:48.447 --> 00:14:50.043
Whoa, cool!"

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:55.320
Fans aren't the only people who hated "Super
Mario Bros."; Hoskins absolutely loathed the

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:58.100
film, and he never kept that disdain to himself.

00:14:58.100 --> 00:14:59.970
He told The Guardian in 2007:

00:14:59.970 --> 00:15:01.399
"The worst thing I ever did?

00:15:01.399 --> 00:15:02.930
'Super Mario Brothers.'

00:15:02.930 --> 00:15:04.480
It was a f---in' nightmare.

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:06.389
The whole experience was a nightmare.

00:15:06.389 --> 00:15:10.839
It had a husband-and-wife team directing,
whose arrogance had been mistaken for talent.

00:15:10.839 --> 00:15:14.670
After so many weeks, their own agent told
them to get off the set!"

00:15:14.670 --> 00:15:18.668
Dismissing them as "idiots," he cited the
project as his biggest regret.

00:15:18.668 --> 00:15:21.459
Matt Damon launched to superstardom with "Good
Will Hunting."

00:15:21.459 --> 00:15:26.910
It brought his career to new heights, and
it helped him land his most iconic role: Jason Bourne.

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:30.390
Damon first played Bourne in "The Bourne Identity"
and has since appeared in three sequels.

00:15:30.390 --> 00:15:34.339
While the movies did exceptionally well and
solidified his place as a Hollywood A-lister,

00:15:34.339 --> 00:15:38.600
Damon isn't a huge fan of the third film,
"The Bourne Ultimatum."

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:42.839
In an interview with GQ, he expressed his
disappointment with the script, which he credited

00:15:42.839 --> 00:15:48.250
to Tony Gilroy's deal to write a single draft,
take no notes or rewrites, and receive, quote,

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:49.850
"an exorbitant amount of money."

00:15:49.850 --> 00:15:53.090
"You can't outrun what you did, Jason.

00:15:53.090 --> 00:15:56.330
You made yourself into who you are."

00:15:56.330 --> 00:16:00.419
Despite pointing out the script as the reason
for the film's lackluster performance, Damon

00:16:00.419 --> 00:16:01.769
doesn't hold Gilroy accountable.

00:16:01.769 --> 00:16:02.769
He explained:

00:16:02.769 --> 00:16:06.819
"I don't blame Tony for taking a boatload
of money and handing in what he handed in.

00:16:06.819 --> 00:16:08.930
It's just that it was unreadable.

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:09.930
This is a career-ender.

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:14.069
I mean, I could put this thing up on eBay,
and it would be game over for that dude.

00:16:14.069 --> 00:16:15.069
It's terrible.

00:16:15.069 --> 00:16:16.069
It's really embarrassing."

00:16:16.069 --> 00:16:20.040
Ultimately, the shooting script was rushed
into production, due to a set release date.

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:25.509
Circa the late '00s, Sam Worthington had become
Hollywood's hottest flavor, landing high-profile

00:16:25.509 --> 00:16:30.279
gigs headlining "Avatar," a "Terminator" sequel,
and a "Clash of the Titans" remake.

00:16:30.279 --> 00:16:31.410
"Tell him.

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:32.550
Tell him."

00:16:32.550 --> 00:16:33.550
"What?"

00:16:33.550 --> 00:16:36.519
"Your journey does not end well.

00:16:36.519 --> 00:16:38.220
Fate has spoken."

00:16:38.220 --> 00:16:43.579
Of course, "Avatar" became a once-in-a-generation
hit, but Worthington's other two projects

00:16:43.579 --> 00:16:45.120
didn't quite work out as well.

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:49.370
"Titans," which put him alongside the likes
of Liam Neeson, Ralph Fiennes, Gemma Arterton,

00:16:49.370 --> 00:16:54.339
Mads Mikkelsen, and other celebrated actors,
did fair-to-middling business, ultimately

00:16:54.339 --> 00:16:57.040
failing to woo critics or audiences.

00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:01.220
The film eventually got a sequel with 2012's
"Wrath of the Titans," but Worthington isn't

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:03.380
a fan of his acting in "Clash."

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:06.270
He apologized to fans in a 2010 interview
with Moviefone, admitting:

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:09.360
"I think the first one, we kind of let down
some people.

00:17:09.360 --> 00:17:12.199
I think I can act f---ing better, to be honest...

00:17:12.199 --> 00:17:15.600
Just take all the notes from people that I
have been reading about on the 'net and give

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:17.329
them a movie they f---ing want."

00:17:18.059 --> 00:17:22.240
When Brad Pitt was cast opposite Tom Cruise
in Anne Rice's eagerly-anticipated "Interview

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:26.310
with the Vampire" adaptation, many saw it
as a passing of the torch.

00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:29.910
Here was Hollywood's hottest young actor matched
with its biggest star.

00:17:29.910 --> 00:17:32.559
In this darkest of tales, who would shine
brightest?

00:17:32.559 --> 00:17:34.530
In the eyes of some, no one.

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:38.059
The movie made money, but according to Pitt,
it was a nightmare to shoot.

00:17:38.059 --> 00:17:42.540
In a 2011 interview with Entertainment Weekly,
Pitt explained how miserable he was, shooting

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:44.160
for six months in the dark.

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:48.770
On top of that, he felt his character wasn't
as fully realized as he should have been.

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:51.750
When asked if he knew what the role would
be going in, Pitt replied:

00:17:51.750 --> 00:17:52.750
"No, I didn't.

00:17:52.750 --> 00:17:53.750
There was no script.

00:17:53.750 --> 00:17:57.630
I knew the book, and in the book, you have
this guy asking, 'Who am I?'

00:17:57.630 --> 00:17:59.860
Which was probably applicable to me at that
time."

00:17:59.860 --> 00:18:02.150
"You thought you could have it all."

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:04.110
"Oh, shut up, Louis."

00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:07.610
Explaining that he got the script two weeks
before shooting, Pitt even tried to get out

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:08.610
of his contract.

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:12.730
He called producer David Geffen, a good friend
of his, and asked what it would cost to break

00:18:12.730 --> 00:18:14.010
the contract.

00:18:14.010 --> 00:18:18.450
When Geffen's answer was in the neighborhood
of $40 million, Pitt decided to take his vitriol

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:21.836
and, like any good vampire, just suck it up.

00:18:21.836 --> 00:18:26.990
2015's "Terminator Genisys" cast Emilia Clarke
as a quasi-new-Linda-Hamilton in the same

00:18:26.990 --> 00:18:28.830
character of Sarah Connor.

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:33.470
The film aimed to reboot the franchise once
again, and had it been successful, Clarke

00:18:33.470 --> 00:18:35.440
would have had a cinematic vehicle for years.

00:18:35.440 --> 00:18:38.750
"Come with me if you want to live."

00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:42.280
Unfortunately, the movie was deemed a disappointment,
even if it did gross more than $440 million

00:18:42.280 --> 00:18:45.650
at the worldwide box office.

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:48.680
Because it underperformed, the planned sequel
was scrapped.

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:53.000
Clarke spoke with Vanity Fair in 2018 about
her experiences filming "Genisys," noting

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:57.000
that she'd seen the film's director Alan Taylor
get "eaten and chewed up on 'Terminator.'"

00:18:57.000 --> 00:18:59.450
"He was not the director I remembered.

00:18:59.450 --> 00:19:00.890
He didn't have a good time.

00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:02.450
No one had a good time."

00:19:02.450 --> 00:19:07.067
On top of that, Clarke expressed relief that
she wouldn't have to make any more "Terminator" films.

00:19:07.067 --> 00:19:11.980
According to the actor, crew on the neighboring
production of 2015’s "Fantastic Four" reboot,

00:19:11.980 --> 00:19:15.950
another infamously troubled shoot, were spotted
wearing jackets that read "AT LEAST WE'RE

00:19:15.950 --> 00:19:17.409
NOT ON 'TERMINATOR'."

00:19:17.409 --> 00:19:21.940
In 2016, Clarke was asked about whether she'd
ever return to the "Terminator" franchise.

00:19:21.940 --> 00:19:22.688
She replied:

00:19:22.688 --> 00:19:23.435
"No.

00:19:23.435 --> 00:19:24.201
Can I say that?

00:19:24.201 --> 00:19:25.070
It's okay.

00:19:25.070 --> 00:19:25.818
No.

00:19:25.818 --> 00:19:26.818
Uh-uh."

00:19:26.958 --> 00:19:28.940
George Clooney: Batman &amp; Robin

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:30.980
If you've ever wondered what the worst "Batman"
movie was, just head into your local comic

00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:35.200
book store, conduct a poll and you'll inevitably
hear a consensus surrounding Joel Schumacher's

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:36.549
last film in the franchise.

00:19:36.549 --> 00:19:40.850
If you happen to pass by the onetime Dark
Knight himself, George Clooney, you could

00:19:40.850 --> 00:19:43.250
ask him, and he'd likely agree.

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:47.320
When Clooney took the role, following Val
Kilmer in Schumacher's "Batman Forever," he

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:51.310
was an on-the-rise TV star navigating a tricky
transition to films.

00:19:51.310 --> 00:19:54.850
At the time, it was the biggest thing he had
ever done, and while he recalls it as the

00:19:54.850 --> 00:19:58.970
role that helped him secure financial freedom,
he nevertheless regrets it more than he does

00:19:58.970 --> 00:20:01.669
being in "Return of the Killer Tomatoes."

00:20:01.669 --> 00:20:06.309
Clooney's Batman had nipples on the bat suit,
a bat-credit card, and dialogue corny enough

00:20:06.309 --> 00:20:08.020
to make Adam West cringe.

00:20:08.020 --> 00:20:11.450
"Let's kick some ice."

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:15.270
Clooney is no fan of the movie and has apologized
to the fans several times.

00:20:15.270 --> 00:20:19.780
During an appearance on "The Graham Norton
Show" in 2016, he remembered the experience

00:20:19.780 --> 00:20:20.780
as a disaster.

00:20:20.780 --> 00:20:23.190
"I always apologize for 'Batman &amp; Robin.'"

00:20:23.190 --> 00:20:26.320
Clooney went on to say that he thought he'd
destroyed the franchise.

00:20:26.320 --> 00:20:30.200
"I thought at the time this was going to be
a very good career move.

00:20:30.761 --> 00:20:32.688
Um, it wasn't."

00:20:32.801 --> 00:20:37.429
Long before donning the Dark Knight's cowl
for the DCEU, Ben Affleck was playing a different

00:20:37.429 --> 00:20:38.909
superhero for Marvel.

00:20:38.909 --> 00:20:43.270
His first foray into costumed crime fighting,
however, didn't receive any encore performances.

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:50.020
Affleck played Matt Murdock, aka Daredevil,
in the 2003 movie, a notorious dud at the time.

00:20:50.020 --> 00:20:53.620
While the film featured notable Easter eggs
for fans who had long hoped to see their hero

00:20:53.620 --> 00:20:58.150
on the silver screen, it deviated from the
source enough to provoke others.

00:20:58.150 --> 00:21:02.040
Affleck's performance isn't terrible, but
the movie features some bad acting, crummy

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:05.780
dialogue, and several scenes that deserve
to reside in the bottom of the discount bin

00:21:05.780 --> 00:21:07.970
at your local 99 cent store.

00:21:07.970 --> 00:21:12.669
In 2015, Affleck admitted to Entertainment
Weekly that the film was a misfire, saying:

00:21:12.669 --> 00:21:14.760
"'Daredevil' didn't work, at all.

00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:18.250
[...] That was before people realized you
could make these movies and make them well.

00:21:18.250 --> 00:21:22.511
There was a cynical sense of 'Put a red leather
outfit on a guy, have him run around, hunt

00:21:22.511 --> 00:21:24.510
some bad guys, and cash the check.'"

